00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.400
As the name Pub/Sub
indicates, Cloud Pub/Sub

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.190
supports a publisher
subscriber model.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:54.280
A publisher application
creates and sends messages

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.040
to a topic which is
a named resource.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.590
And the messages are
stored until acknowledged

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.540
by all subscribers.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.599
To receive these messages,
a subscriber application

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.310
creates a subscription
to a topic.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.040
The subscriber
receives a message

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.320
by either Cloud
Pub/Sub pushing them

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.690
to the subscribers
chosen endpoint

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:16.890
or by subscriber pulling
them from the service.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:19.690
When the message is
acknowledged by the subscriber,

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.240
it is removed from the
subscription backlog

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.310
and not delivered again.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.460
The message itself can contain
the payload and optional

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.150
attributes that describe
the payload content.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.060
Communication can be
one too many also called

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:40.360
fan out, many to one or
fan in, and many to many,

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.140
which means if subscriber
x wants to receive messages

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.870
from two different
publishers, a and b,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.470
it can be achieved by creating
two subscriptions corresponding

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.500
to the topics for
each publisher, which

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.790
is a many-to-one model.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.030
Similarly, if two
different applications

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:01.430
want to subscribe to the same
message from a publisher,

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:03.160
then it can be
achieved by creating

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.460
two different subscriptions
from the same topic, which

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.479
is a one-to-many model.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.040
Now, remember Cat-Out, the order
processing and delivery service

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.820
from the last episode?

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:16.210
Let's see how Pub/Sub
topic and subscriptions

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.750
would be set up for this
asynchronous functionality.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.870
The order service sends the
messages to Pub/Sub topic

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.040
where they get stored.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.870
The packaging and
notification service

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:29.260
subscribe to these messages
through the respective Pub/Sub

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.140
subscription in
a fanout fashion,

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.380
Packaging service will send
messages to Pub/Sub topic.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.120
And the shipping and
notification service

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.600
will subscribe to the
respective Pub/Sub subscription.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.720
Similarly, the shipping
service will send the messages

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.830
to Pub/Sub topic.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.510
And the order and
notification service

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:48.280
will subscribe to their
respective Pub/Sub

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.570
subscriptions.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.600
Now, Cat-Out is going
to a global expansion

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:55.390
with packaging service in
Europe and order processing

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:56.800
service in US.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.500
Because Cloud Pub/Sub
is a global service,

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.200
it ensures that messages
get from one location

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.110
to another no matter
where these services are

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.020
with consistent latency and
no replication necessary.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.140
This is huge for
the Cat-Out team,

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:13.600
because they can focus
on developing the service

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.900
and leave the global
scaling to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:19.540
What do we need to know about
publisher and subscriber

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.600
endpoints?

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.760
Well, let's see.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.980
Publishers can be
any application

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.290
that can make https
request to Google APIs.com.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.230
An App Engine app, a web service
hosted on Google Compute Engine

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.620
or any other third-party
network and install an app

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:39.350
for desktop or mobile
devices or even a browser.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.730
Pull subscribers can
also be any application

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:46.900
that can make https
requests to Google APIs.com,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.290
although Push subscribers must
be web hook end points that

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.590
can accept Post
requests over https.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.070
Cloud Pub/Sub is all
about service integration.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.560
Data is ingested
into Cloud Pub/Sub

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.500
from the front-end services
databases and other event

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.410
producers, then received
by Cloud Dataflow

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.660
or our other event
processing service

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.750
for de-duplication,
ordering, and enrichment.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.550
From Cloud Dataflow,
the event can

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.500
be sent to multiple targets.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:17.680
It can be used to facilitate
end user experiences,

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.860
like managing notification
via Firebase notifications

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.420
and messaging, or storing
end user state in Firestore

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.060
or managing an IT device.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.850
Other targets could be data
warehouse or data lakes,

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.870
like BigQuery and GCS or
event-driven and [? sololess ?]

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.770
apps, like Cloud Functions,
App Engine, and Cloud Run.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.360
Bigtable could
also be your target

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.660
for operational
databases or just caches.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.940
Cloud Pub/Sub has
lots of different use

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:49.600
cases, which can be categorized
into two broad buckets.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.750
It's great for streaming
analytics or ingestion of data

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.640
into analytical systems.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.060
It's also great for implementing
asynchronous workflows,

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.950
like the order
processing application

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:03.080
we saw earlier in
the Cat-Out example.

